<u>26-AUG-2005 11:39 AM (Hong Kong Time)</u><br>
Last night after the junk boat night cruise, I met and conversed with my first fellow hosteller. His name was Young. He was from NYC but quit his job and is travelling the world for one year. How cool.  He currently lives in Thailand in a smaller town and told me things to watch out for and common phrases to know in BKK.  He told me how cheap it is to live in BKK.  His new scooter costs $1000 and he pays $250/month for rent + $25 for all utilities.  One can own a house for as little as $30K-$40K.  Young had been spending time in HK and just left work in NYC recently after saving up enough $$$ and moved out to Chiang Mai, Thailand.  He's got a Thai girlfriend and is learning Thai himself.  He's currently also travelling throughout Asia and made Chiang Mai his basecamp.  He had spent time in HKK shopping for digital cameras and pirated DVDs.
<p>
On this morning, M and I decided to relax and wake up a little late and then pack for the flight out to BKK.  The last few days of walking around the city have actually not made us that hungry and today was no exception.  We weren't hungry  enough for breakfast.
<p>
I decided to go to the uber slow computer at the hostel and write an update and try to upload some pics.  However, it was like working with an old 8088 computer from back in the day with a 300 baud modem connection (I swear!).
<p>
During the slow picture upload, another hosteller by the name of Marco came in.  He had been referring to himself as Marco Polo because the Chinese knew how to say that.  Marco was from the US, specifically from San Francisco.  He had been working for Yahoo and decided to just take a leave of absence and travel throughout Asia for a year not caring if Yahoo didn't hire him back.  He had budgetted $2K/month to live on (including airfare) which if you cut out all the thrills and live in hostels and eat like the locals, you could survive on.
<p>
Anyway, Marco had explained to me how HK was his first stop.  He told me he was going to Vietnam, Laos, Cambodia, and many other Asian countries.  Bangkok, too, was his next destination.  I mentioned that's where I was headed to next and when my flight left.  He said that was his flight too.  We decided to head out together.
<p>
We got M and her stuff and headed to the MTR.  We had tickets but Marco didn't.  So we went ahead and waited for him.  He got his ticket but had passed us up due to the congestion at the train terminal.  We went after him but saw him jump over the gate to get on the train without paying.  I had hoped to see him on the plane but we searched when we got on and I never saw him again.
